KSEF_CorrectionAbandonedSiblingUnsent=Invoice %s already has the correction %s, abandoned and never sent to KSeF. A second correction would count the payments twice: reopen %s and modify it instead (Reopen, then Modify)
KSEF_CorrectionAbandonedSiblingSent=Invoice %s is already corrected in KSeF by %s (abandoned in Dolibarr). Make the new correction of %s instead
KSEF_CreditNoteUsedCannotDraft=Credit note %s cannot go back to draft or be deleted: its credit is already used by %s. Remove the credit from that invoice first
KSEF_CorrectionExcessUsed=Correction %s cannot be undone: the excess credit it gave the customer is already used by %s. Remove the credit from that invoice first
KSEF_CorrectionExcessSplit=Correction %s cannot be undone: its excess credit was split and the pieces add up to more than that credit, so they cannot be told apart from other credit of the same invoice. Remove the pieces of this credit by hand, then undo the correction
KSEF_CorrectionAbandonUnsent=Correction %s was never sent to KSeF and cannot be abandoned: set it back to draft (Modify) and delete it, which puts the corrected invoice back as it was
KSEF_DepositCorrectionAbandonSent=Correction %s of a deposit invoice is in KSeF and cannot be abandoned: a correction sent to KSeF is undone only by another correction
KSEF_DepositFinalInAccounting=The credit of deposit invoice %s changed, but %s is transferred to accounting: its deposit deduction was left as it is. Make a correction of it, or remove it from the ledger first
KSEF_CreditNotePositiveRefused=Credit note %s cannot be validated: its total is positive. A credit note reduces an invoice, so its amounts must be negative; to raise an amount, use Corrective invoice instead
KSEF_DepositCreditNoteRateBelowZero=Credit note %s cannot be validated: it lowers the part of deposit invoice %s at VAT rate %s below zero. A credit note can lower each VAT rate of the deposit at most to zero; check its lines and VAT rates.
KSEF_DepositKorektaRateBelowZero=Correction %s cannot be validated: it lowers deposit invoice %s at VAT rate %s below zero. A deposit correction can lower each VAT rate of the deposit at most to zero (it cannot give back VAT the deposit never charged); check its lines and VAT rates.
KSEF_ImportErrorKorZalBelowZero=This corrective invoice takes deposit invoice %s at VAT rate %s below zero. A corrective invoice of a deposit can lower each VAT rate at most to zero (it cannot give back VAT the deposit never charged), so it is not imported. Ask the supplier to correct it
KSEF_CorrectionCreditRecovered=%s: %s of the excess credit that the below-zero correction of %s gave is taken back (its negative part)
KSEF_CorrectionCreditNotRecovered=%s: %s more than its total is due, because that much of the credit the below-zero correction of %s gave is already used.
KSEF_CorrectionRecoveredCreditUsed=Correction %s cannot be undone: part of the excess credit it took back is now used by %s. Remove the credit from that invoice first
KSEF_CorrectionRecoveredCreditChanged=Correction %s cannot be undone: the excess credit it took part of was split or changed since. Put that credit back as it was first
